const blogPost1Image =
    "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1515705576963-95cad62945b6?q=80&force=true&w=1920";
const blogPost1Header = "LinkedIn Introduction - Friday 29th August 2025";
const blogPost1Body =
    "Hi there everyone! I figured it would be a good time for a reintroduction so you get to know me better and my career ambitions. My name is Thomas Popham (he/him) and I’m a postgraduate in MSc. Computer Science (June 2023) from Teesside University. I’d best describe myself as eager, curious and deeply supportive of other graduates like myself; looking to make their mark within software development. Following my experience during university, I have since developed academic and personal experience in technologies such as: React/React Native, Flutter and Android Compose front-end (web and mobile) development, .NET and Spring Boot for back-end development, database management with SQL, PostgreSQL and Google Firestore, as well as Blender and Unreal Engine experience for 3D Animation Rigging. I firmly believe that life-long learning is one of my main driving forces for my career; spurring me to develop my skillset and experience in areas of technology I’m most passionate about. Recently, I have been improving my knowledge and experience within full-stack development and animation development in line with my passion for supporting children in their educational journey. My ultimate career goal is to develop educational games/immersive software for young children based on impactful academic research and to make a meaningful positive difference to their educational development and pedagogy. I’ve looked into existing research into the effective adoption of technology (e.g. Augmented Reality or Artificial Intelligence) for neurodiverse individuals and how it can be effective in supporting children with Autism or Autism with ADHD. In my spare time, I’m passionate for literature (currently reading Piranesi by Susanna Clarke), watching movies (especially the Studio Ghibli films), listening to music (ELO, Rush and David Bowie are my favourites) and some creative drawing/animation. I firmly believe that creativity is a critical driving force for a child’s lifelong development and forms my overall approach to problem solving, project planning and software design and development. Enabling me to \"think-outside-the-box\" and share a research-based perspective that I would love to bring to the workplace. If you need any technical support or you wish to give any career advice with me, please feel free to share! I’m more than happy to connect with other developers (especially other computer graduates like myself!) and to share knowledge, experience and advice through LinkedIn. Over the next few months, I will be sharing updates on personal projects I will be actively working on along with any technical support I could share with other graduates like myself. You're also more than welcome to reshare this post with your network! It’s great meeting you all and I look forward to connecting with you! Thanks!";
const blogPost1ImageCredit = "Photo by Zoltan Tasi on Unsplash";


const blogPost2Image = "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1460518451285-97b6aa326961?q=80&force=true&w=1920";
const blogPost2Header = "Project Update - dreamStudy - Tuesday 17th March 2026";
const blogPost2Body = "Hey there everyone! Thought I might share some of what I’ve been working on lately (keep in mind, it's VERY early in development!) with you all. I’ve been lately been inspired by the work done by Genio and Arbor Education and how they’ve supported students in their educational development and gave teachers the tools to best support them. My full-stack project (called dreamStudy) is being built specifically for SEND (Special Educational Needs and Disabilities) students who are taking their GCSE and A Level examinations and to best support their revision. I plan to do this through the incorporation of key accessibility tools like an built-in AAC, filters for colour-blind support, dyslexia-friendly text fonts and hand gesture control that can best support their learning. I firmly believe that NO child or young person should ever be left behind in their learning purely because of their disability or needs and this project that I am currently building aims to achieve this. I also wanted to use this project as an opportunity to further develop my skills within React Native as my main front-end framework of choice for both web (yes web!) and mobile devices. I’ve also looked into incorporating Skia as a powerful graphical renderer that can display modern and performant UI components that enhance the user’s overall experience with the website or app. You might have heard of Skia used as the main renderer for Flutter (before it was replaced by Impeller), but thanks to Shopify, it has been reincorporated to work with React Native. This makes it an ideal library for rendering graphics and animations, making it much more performant for an app where 60fps performance is key in certain UX cases! Any React Native developers, SEND teachers and experts in the field are more than welcome to share feedback with me on where key improvements and recommendations should be made to the development of the project: https://lnkd.in/eHqgM2x5. I'll be sharing more (when I have time) on any other projects that I will be working on. Thank you very much! 😀👍 一緒に夢に飛び込みましょう (Let’s dive into our dreams together)";
const blogPost2ImageCredit = "Photo by Alexis Brown on Unsplash";


const blogPost3Image = "https://images.unsplash.com/uploads/1413387158190559d80f7/6108b580?q=80&force=true&w=1920";
const blogPost3Header = "Job Update For Recruiters - Monday 4th May 2026";
const blogPost3Body = "Hey there everyone! Hope you all have been having a fantastic bank holiday weekend! Just a small update on where I am planning to work for any IT or software recruiters that are interested. I am looking to work either On-Site or Hybrid in any of the following locations: York, Leeds, Manchester, Liverpool, Sheffield, Bristol, Southampton and Bournemouth. I am also more than happy to relocate for any positions based in these areas where necessary. More posts will be coming this month on progress on my dreamStudy project and other work and ideas in React Native, Flutter, Unreal Engine and Blender. Thank you very much! 😀👍 一緒に夢に飛び込みましょう (Let’s dive into our dreams together)";
const blogPost3ImageCredit = "Photo by Charles Forerunner on Unsplash";


const blogPost4Image = "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1542626991-cbc4e32524cc?q=80&force=true&w=1920";
const blogPost4Header = "Update - dreamStudy, dreamTravel And Future Projects - Friday 3rd July 2026";
const blogPost4Body = "Hey there everyone! Sorry it’s been a while! I’ve had my neck deep in job applications, emails and family commitments lately, and haven’t yet given an update on the projects I’m working on in-between job applications. Two projects that best demonstrate my experience in full-stack development and mobile application development are dreamTravel, a travel booking platform designed with personalisation in mind, and dreamStudy, a GCSE and A Level revision support platform designed for SEND students. Throughout the development of these two projects, I’ve learnt so much on how to best manage state locally and globally, rendering between mobile and web, and ensuring that the UI is fully interactable with accessibility tools. Any React Native and Flutter developers/graduates are more than welcome to contribute to these projects and share any ideas and recommendations with me if they have free time to spare (See the GitHub links in the comments). I’m also researching for another project called dreamBuilder (an educational AR game built for teaching key STEM skills and computational thinking to children in primary school years 3 to 6) after both dreamTravel and dreamStudy are more feature complete and functional. Sometime later this month, I’ll be publishing two blog articles on graduate careers support and STEM education coming up on Medium and my blog (maybe August if I’m too busy). Hope you all have a fantastic summer (hopefully not too hot!🥵) and keep dreaming! Thank you very much!😀👍 一緒に夢に飛び込みましょう (Let’s dive into our dreams together)";
const blogPost4ImageCredit = "Photo by Jo Szczepanska on Unsplash";


const blogPost5Image = "assets/druridge_bay_photo_2026.jpg";
const blogPost5Header = "Update - Great North Run and the Leeds Digital Festival - Friday 18th September 2026";
const blogPost5Body = "Hey there everyone! Hope you’ve all had a fantastic summer break! I’ve had my hands full lately with applications, networking events, projects and EVEN the Great North Run to cheer on my younger brother running his first half-marathon for Diabetes UK (SUPER proud of you my man!). But I have been making strong progress where it counts. Funny thing is, I often come up with ideas for new projects in the middle of working on one, and it can admittedly be frustrating when I and other graduates think about the quantity of projects we worked on, rather than thinking about the quality of them. I’d imagine graduates will have had this feeling before and I can’t surely be the only one!😂 I’m really looking forward to the Leeds Digital Festival next week and getting to network with others, engage in coding challenges and getting my name out there in the industry. One event that I definitely recommend to any graduates that might have left Uni this year or last year is the Tech Careers Launchpad hosted by Corecom Tech Academy. Any graduates or juniors that might be struggling to stand out in a market that is highly competitive might find some valuable life lessons and advice from others who lived through it to the other side. If you are interested in joining, take a look at the event link in the comment I posted below. Thank you very much and I can’t wait to see you all there!😀👍 一緒に夢に飛び込みましょう (Let’s dive into our dreams together)";
const blogPost5ImageCredit = "Photo was taken at Druridge Bay Beach in Northumberland";


const blogPost6Image = "https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:4800/0*bBPpU7qhGsUENOxI";
const blogPost6Header = "Article - Some Rambling Thoughts From My Experience At Leeds Digital Festival 2026 - Thursday 1st October 2026";
const blogPost6Body = "How can I prove my value if I lack experience? Where can AI gain the most value in a business? Is faster delivery really the kind of productivity that companies are looking for? How valuable is my part-time role as a form of commercial experience? What hidden risks should I look for in UX design? How can AI help with collaboration between designers and engineers? These are a just a few of the questions that have been shared over the course of the Leeds Digital Festival this year. I came back from my time there last week and had already learnt so much from project managers, AI managers, UX designers and even other graduates like myself. Key members from Reed, Skipton Group and FDM Group shared their past experiences during an early careers talk at Leeds Beckett University. The stories shared during this I feel, are insightful for many graduates who feel lost amidst a competitive jobs market. Where people-skills matter just as much as the technical ones, and any form of experience (whether placements, part-time roles, internships and academic and personal projects) is just as valuable as commercial experience. Any recent graduates who might be struggling, IT service desk/support/technician roles could act as a strong stepping stone for getting into IT, in whatever industry you prefer. On the topic of AI, I attended the AI Lab hosted by Zygens, who shared an interactive demonstration on generative AI tools (Cursor, OpenClaw and Railway) and their value within a business. Many others who were there, just so happen to be founding their own AI start-up businesses and gained plenty of useful advice and ideas. I’ve learnt a lot from the questions raised regarding AI governance within businesses and considered how graduates and juniors can potentially benefit from AI adoption when they begin working within software development. For me, it can help gain a unique advantage in automating specific tasks that take up too much time away from the innovation and problem solving that businesses tend to look for in graduates and juniors. From the discussions raised, speed is NOT a substitute for productivity in software delivery and product management. Understanding where to apply it and its value gained from its adoption will help businesses in making the right decision on AI. Different software features can be deployed rapidly, but what client is going to use them all? This question appeared in the UX Design presentation, where Andy Curry and Paul Cook from LION+MASON and Nate Langley from Monzo have shared their experiences with AI automation and how it has helped with their productivity. Hearing real-life examples can help to set the correct expectations around AI and to better understand its value in technology and product management. Also, the talk shared by Andy Curry on research-based and inclusive design has helped me to understand and apply these UX principles to front-end development and how design and engineering teams should best work with each other to achieve this. Any graduates who are interested, I highly recommend getting in touch with some of these companies and to ask the questions that can best prepare you for the career that you rightfully deserve. Thanks! 😀👍 一緒に夢に飛び込みましょう (Let’s dive into our dreams together)";
const blogPost6ImageCredit = "Photo by jimylloyd laumain on Unsplash";


const blogPost7Image = "https://framerusercontent.com/assets/0Lx3ZCm4gLOEY3lcUm2KQBmP4.png";
const blogPost7Header = "Update - Work Experience At OpenCast in Newcastle - Thursday 15th October 2026";
const blogPost7Body = "Hey there everyone! I came back from a 3 day work experience workshop at Opencast in Newcastle upon Tyne and just had an insightful and engaging time learning from professionals in tech consulting. For me, I had the excellent opportunity to know the different departments, roles and responsibilities of tech consultancy. As well as having the opportunity to apply mentoring skills in a pair programming workshop, supporting others in building key problem solving skills that can set them up for life. Any uni students, graduates, career switchers or anyone looking to get into tech and gain career and interview support, I definitely recommend getting stuck into events like this as an opportunity share your passion for tech. A BIG BIG BIG thanks to everyone at Opencast for setting up this workshop and sharing valuable career and interview support with all of us! Many thanks for reading! 😀👍 一緒に夢に飛び込みましょう (Let’s dive into our dreams together)";
const blogPost7ImageCredit = "Photo by OpenCast at opencastsoftware.com";


const blogPost8Image = "https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:4800/0*7tuMJIUNxouBPdqf";
const blogPost8Header = "Article - Helpful Resources For Any Graduates Preparing For Upcoming Assessments/Tests - Monday 19th October 2026";
const blogPost8Body = "Hey there everyone! Any graduates out there who are unsure of how to effectively prepare for any upcoming assessments since graduation, I have some recommended practice resources that will help you succeed in these tests. They can be pretty frightening with their tight deadlines and unfamiliar structure but, with enough practice, you can ace these before prepping for any interviews that might come after. There is also no shame in refreshing your knowledge in your GCSE foundations (it’s been a while anyway!) prior to any numerical and verbal reasoning tests you’ll get down the road. Graduates First — An assessment practice platform highly recommended by many universities in preparing for numerical, verbal, logical and other cognitive assessments. A MUST for any Uni students graduating and preparing for future applications. https://lnkd.in/exF_pMVR. Sova Assessments — A common assessment platform used by many companies in evaluating graduates based on their skills. The website has a practice hub for those who have upcoming assessments to complete that I highly recommend looking at to get used to the structure of Sova Assessment. https://lnkd.in/e2b8bvRW. Assessment Day — Another practice platform like Graduates First that offers practice assessments based on actual assessments that many companies employ during graduate recruitment. https://lnkd.in/e56EUdQd. HackerRank — Ideal for computer science/computing graduates (like me!) practicing their coding skills and preparing for any upcoming coding tests or interviews in a wide range of programming languages including Java, C#, Python, TypeScript with React, etc. This platform also offers practice technical interviews based on coding questions and scenarios presented in real-life interviews. There are also some AI and agentic practice tools that can also help you future-proof your career in software engineering, giving you a unique advantage amongst others. https://lnkd.in/e5QNtdSH. Brilliant — A fantastic learning platform that I highly recommend trying during your coffee breaks. It’s similar to Duolingo in its structure and overall presentation, but it’s based around developing your STEM and problem solving skills. Enabling you to sharpen your foundations and bring them forward in your career. https://brilliant.org/. Hopefully these resources should help calm those initial nerves before preparing for any upcoming assessments and tests, as it did for me lately. Thank you very much for reading and I wish you all the very best of luck in your upcoming assessments! 😀👍 一緒に夢に飛び込みましょう (Let’s dive into our dreams together)";
const blogPost8ImageCredit = "Photo by Nick Morrison on Unsplash";
